WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.520
- A lot of words to describe you.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.410
Social media mogul, entrepreneur,
internet personality,

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:06.243
all this stuff.

00:00:06.243 --> 00:00:08.080
How would you describe yourself?

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.453
- I think I'm an entrepreneur.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.000
Somebody asked me today, in an interview,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.890
what do I do with my downtime?

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:17.030
I told them that I start other businesses.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:19.350
This is my hobby. This is my passion.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.210
It's what I love to do.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.470
I love building businesses,
this is fun for me.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.550
You don't understand, I'm
a pure bred entrepreneur

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.263
for real, for real, for
real, like, documented.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:31.830
- How did this all start?

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:32.960
- What happened with me is,

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.410
I had the great benefit
of being an immigrant.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.160
I was born in Belarus, in
the former Soviet Union,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.820
and came to the States
in '78, set up in Queens.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.730
And my parents lived the American dream.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:44.750
They worked very hard.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.350
My dad was a stock boy in a liquor store

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.075
in Clark, New Jersey, and eventually saved

00:00:48.075 --> 00:00:51.150
up enough money to buy a store
in Springfield, New Jersey.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.070
I was lemonade stands, baseball
cards, real hustler kid,

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:55.210
anything to make a buck.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.450
And at 14, I got dragged into the store.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.030
Heard the internet, my
freshman year of college,

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.610
heard that sound, (garbled noise).

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:04.300
Knew that it was special.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.594
And in 1996, I launched
one of the first e-commerce

00:01:06.594 --> 00:01:09.500
wine businesses in America,
called Winelibrary.com.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.850
Took over my dad's business,
kind of running it day to day

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.920
in 1998 alongside with him.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.530
And from '98 to 2003
helped grow that business

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:18.662
from a three to a $60 million business.

00:01:18.662 --> 00:01:19.780
(air whooshing)

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.627
- [Narrator] But growing a
business from three to 60 million

00:01:21.627 --> 00:01:23.170
was just the beginning.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.320
Let's see what Gary created
after leaving Wine Library.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.570
- I was 34 years old--
- Yeah.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:33.150
- When I started VaynerMedia,
I had spent the 12 best years

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.550
of my youth building my
dad's liquor store for him.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:39.120
I left that business, had
no money, had no equity.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.030
I was worthless to the bank.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.880
VaynerMedia started in
somebody else's conference room

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.050
'cause we had no money for rent.

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:48.260
So it was a ton of risk
by normal standards

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:49.980
when somebody reads it on paper.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.713
But not necessarily for me,
it just felt like progression.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:56.810
I run VaynerMedia.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:00.850
We are an 800 person firm,
$100 million in revenue

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.410
in four years from zero.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.450
We worked with GE and
Pepsi and Under Armour

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.970
and Toyota and the biggest
brands in the world.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:09.590
- What is VaynerMedia do?

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:10.980
- We're a modern day Mad Men.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:12.500
We're a Madison Avenue agency.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.370
The same people that used
to sell commercial time

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.220
on anything you ever did
in radio and television,

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.689
we now do that on Facebook,
Snapchat, Instagram,

00:02:19.689 --> 00:02:23.270
and we produced the
creative for the brands

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.780
to sell stuff through the phone.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.489
We are living through a revolution.

00:02:27.489 --> 00:02:32.489
This my friends, this is the television.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:37.660
And the television is
the radio, and it's 1960.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:39.280
And there's a huge opportunity.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.300
- What does being a
contemporary communicator mean?

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.500
- It means that you understand where

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.064
people are paying attention,
whether that's Twitch,

00:02:48.064 --> 00:02:52.000
or Tiktok, or believe it or
not, organic reach on LinkedIn.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.010
And then are you capable
of creating the creative,

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.586
the videos, the pictures,
the written words,

00:02:56.586 --> 00:02:59.610
that are contextual to those platforms

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.398
to make the agenda success.

00:03:02.398 --> 00:03:04.360
- [Narrator] VaynerMedia
grew into a global

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:08.070
advertising agency with five
offices across New York,

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:12.220
London, Singapore Los Angeles,
and Chattanooga, Tennessee.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:15.100
As time went on Gary created Vayner X,

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.340
a modern communications holding company,

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:18.720
that houses different ventures

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.310
like the Gallery Media
Group, the Sasha group,

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.410
Vayner Productions, Tracer,

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:26.190
Vayner Commerce and Vayner Speakers.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.540
Gary also continues to
build other businesses.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.330
After four years of building Resy,

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:33.040
a restaurant reservation
app, it sold for nine figures

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.350
to American express in 2019.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:38.500
Gary's also an extremely
strong business collaborator.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.740
In 2017 he became the face of K Swiss.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.181
His collaboration helped contribute

00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:47.010
to their $200 million plus exit in 2019.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.910
- Shopping cart below, if
you're not buying this,

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:50.260
you're breaking my heart.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:52.243
- [Narrator] He's also a
co-founder of Empathy Wines

00:03:52.243 --> 00:03:55.350
which was recently acquired
by Constellation Brands.

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.910
- You just created Empathy last year.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:00.620
- Yeah, this is one of those
one plus one equals 11.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:03.432
Where both sides of the
equation brings so much value.

00:04:03.432 --> 00:04:06.659
- [Narrator] CEO, Gary is
the Chief Executive Officer

00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:10.240
of Vayner X, but how would
Gary describe what he does?

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.230
- At its most pure sense, I
am an operating entrepreneur.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:15.950
- [Narrator] So, let's take a deeper look

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.823
into what drives Gary,
and how he operates.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:23.470
- I'm a kid who started working literally,

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.490
the day I left college.

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:28.500
I walked into Wine Library
about six o'clock that night,

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.940
worked for two hours, then went home.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.200
And literally from that moment,

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.190
literally the day I left college,

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:35.130
to the second I'm sitting here right now,

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:39.500
I have never not been the
chief operator of a business.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.300
And I've made payroll every single week

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.720
since I was 22 years old.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.230
And I'm proud of that because
it's practical as fuck.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:50.730
I've taken VaynerMedia as the
CEO and COO, for that matter,

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.090
I have operated it every day.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.520
I've taken it from zero
to $200 million in revenue

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.920
with no cash infusion, started
it in a conference room.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:00.418
It's now my second company that I built.

00:05:00.418 --> 00:05:04.320
And what I want to do is
inspire two 14 year old girls

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.940
in Kansas city right now to
build a billion dollar company

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.390
on having a bunch of employees

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:10.910
hugging each other in the halls.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:14.800
And it is not lost on me
that I am taking the gifts

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.630
that my mom and dad gave me

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:18.670
and I'm giving them to the world.

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:22.930
And my happiness framework,
and the way I treat people,

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.660
is for some reason becoming cool.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:28.460
And if, and I'm just gonna
squeeze the shit out of that

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.330
because it's leading to good.

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:34.200
I know I'm onto something
so fundamentally different.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.290
And I know that I can
inspire people to do it

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.910
'cause it's gonna have
the empire part too.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:41.340
See, that's when people don't get.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.740
The reason I'm gonna win,
is because I've got both.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:47.960
The honey part means that you
want to go change the world.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:51.230
The empire part means
you'll do it at all costs.

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:52.090
I'm neither.

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:53.620
- [Narrator] Gary has a natural ability

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:56.330
to see consumer trends,
which led him to be an early

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:59.770
investor in companies like
Uber, Twitter, Tumblr,

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:01.270
Facebook, Snapchat, and Venmo.

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:02.103
(air whooshing)

00:06:02.103 --> 00:06:05.500
- I am predicting that
Facebook acquires Instagram,

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:07.450
15 million users for Instagram,

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:09.150
probably gonna push 30 next year.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:10.540
And I just have this funny feeling

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:12.420
that the Zuk's gonna look at that talent,

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:13.253
look at that app, bring it in house.

00:06:13.253 --> 00:06:15.720
(air whooshing)
So I was on Pierce Morgan.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.756
His opening line is, "This
company is 550 days old.

00:06:18.756 --> 00:06:21.320
How the hell is it worth
a billion dollars?"

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:23.800
And my opening line is, "They stole it."

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.510
I get out of the studio, and
literally there are thousands

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:29.520
of tweets of people
calling me a fucking idiot.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:32.156
And then I guess last
year this time, right,

00:06:32.156 --> 00:06:35.250
Facebook bought WhatsApp for 18 billion.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:36.940
And every article like the next day

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:39.120
after the news came out, is
like, "Oh, wait a minute.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.470
They fucking stole Instagram."

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:42.630
- [Narrator] And more recently,

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:43.738
Gary predicted this acquisition.

00:06:43.738 --> 00:06:44.610
(air whooshing)

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:46.770
- When Joe Rogan inevitably announces

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:49.480
that he paid a hundred
million dollars to exclusively

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:52.120
be on whatever pop
platform he's gonna go on,

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:53.667
I have a funny feeling
everyone's gonna be like,

00:06:53.667 --> 00:06:54.500
"Wait a minute."

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:58.340
- I have an announcement. The
podcast is moving to Spotify.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.000
- [Narrator] Gary's also
involved in a variety

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.390
of organizations, like Charity
Water, Pencils of Promise,

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:06.180
Bojangles' MikMak, the All In Challenge,

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:09.030
the Minnesota Rokkr,
VaynerSports, and more.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:10.670
Now, let's take a deeper look

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:13.381
into Gary's personal brand, @GaryVee.

00:07:13.381 --> 00:07:16.050
- [Woman] Gary Vaynerchuk, AKA, Gary Vee,

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.410
is a self-help media mogul.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.611
- [Narrator] One of Gary's
core beliefs as a marketer,

00:07:20.611 --> 00:07:23.040
is that every company and human being,

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.080
is a media company first.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:27.670
Because of that, he built Team GaryVee.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:30.594
A team of strategists,
video editors, writers,

00:07:30.594 --> 00:07:33.610
and designers, to help him
grow his personal brand.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:35.783
Gary's been putting out
content for 10 plus years

00:07:35.783 --> 00:07:38.600
across the platforms at the time.

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.850
Starting with wine content,
with Wine Library TV.

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:44.165
Then he moved to hosting
the Ask Gary Vee Show,

00:07:44.165 --> 00:07:46.960
a Q and A show between
Gary and his audience.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.180
Then, he started documenting
his life's journey

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:50.751
through DailyVee and WeeklyVee.

00:07:50.751 --> 00:07:51.930
(air whooshing)
- Like, it's so obvious

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:53.150
to me what's gonna happen with DailyVee.

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:54.800
It's gonna be so fucking crazy,

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:56.659
what it all looks like a year from today.

00:07:56.659 --> 00:07:57.560
(air whooshing)

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:59.772
- [Narrator] And he also started
a live stream Q and A show

00:07:59.772 --> 00:08:01.560
called Tea with GaryVee.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.770
Gary is also a five times New York times

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:06.340
best selling author and
travels across the world

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:09.890
for Keynotes and has a top
business podcast where he talks

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:13.730
to some of the biggest
entrepreneurs, investors, artists

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:15.787
and thought leaders of our time.

00:08:15.787 --> 00:08:18.537
(dramatic music)

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:56.676
- When Gary Vaynerchuk retires
or goes off in the sunset,

00:08:56.676 --> 00:08:58.720
what do you want to be remembered for?

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.067
- That I gave more than I took.

00:09:01.067 --> 00:09:04.150
I really want to be remembered for that.

